<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Indian National Army</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1942">1942</a>–<a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Azad Hind Fauj</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Indian National Army was a force raised with Japanese support, largely from Indian prisoners of war captured in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaya">Malaya</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a>, to fight British rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Revived under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subhas-chandra-bose">Subhas Chandra Bose</a> in 1943, it served as the army of Azad Hind and fought in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imphal">Imphal</a> and Kohima campaign of 1944. The trials of its officers in 1945–1946 stirred nationalist feeling across India.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/indian-national-army.jpg" alt="Indian National Army" width="320" height="415" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Adiiitya · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:INA_Insignia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and South-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a> were refuges for exiled Indian nationalists. Japan sent intelligence missions into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a>, notably under Major Iwaichi Fujiwara, whose F Kikan made contact with Indian nationalists in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaya">Malaya</a>. About 70,000 Indian troops, mostly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a>, were stationed in Malaya, and in the Malayan Campaign many were captured, nearly 45,000 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fall-of-singapore">fall of Singapore</a> alone. From these prisoners the first Indian National Army was formed under Captain Mohan Singh. He refused to align it with the Japanese and resigned after ordering it to disband, which ended the first INA.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subhas-chandra-bose">Subhas Chandra Bose</a> reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a> from Nazi Germany in May 1943, and on 4 July he took over the Indian Independence League and the Second INA. Around 18,000 Indian civilians in the region also enlisted. On 23 October 1943 Azad Hind declared war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. Bose insisted that the INA should rank as an allied army in the Japanese offensive towards Manipur rather than serve only for espionage and propaganda. Its 1st Division, under Shah Nawaz Khan, advanced through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gurung">Tamu</a> towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imphal">Imphal</a> and Kohima, but the offensive was halted, and in the withdrawal many INA soldiers died of starvation.</p><p>In 1945 the INA took part in the defence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>, and surviving units surrendered at various places in early April. Nearly 6,000 troops under A. D. Loganathan remained in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangon">Rangoon</a> and surrendered as the city fell. The INA has been described as a collaborationist force, and its battlefield performance was poor.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Of the INA's 43,000 recruits, 16,000 were captured, and the British charged 300 officers with treason. Between November 1945 and May 1946 about ten courts-martial were held in public at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-fort">Red Fort</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>. Claude Auchinleck hoped they would turn opinion against the INA, but the first accused, Prem Sahgal, Gurubaksh Singh Dhillon and Shah Nawaz Khan, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a>, a Muslim and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a>, were acclaimed by many Indians as patriots. Philip Mason wrote that in a matter of weeks the INA were acclaimed as heroes who fought for the freedom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-congress">Indian National Congress</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/all-india-muslim-league">Muslim League</a> both made the release of the prisoners a political issue.</p><p>Several INA veterans later held public office: Mohan Singh sat in the Rajya Sabha, Shah Nawaz Khan was Minister of State for Rail in the first Indian cabinet, and Lakshmi Sahgal, Minister for Women's Affairs in the Azad Hind government, became a public figure in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Indian National Army” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Subhas Chandra Bose led an <b>Indian National Army</b> alongside Japan, and the Bengal famine of 1943 discredited the government.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-raj">British Raj · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Subhas Chandra Bose (1897–1945), twice Congress president, broke with Gandhi in 1939, escaped from house arrest in 1941 and led the <b>Indian National Army</b>, raised with Japanese support largely from Indian prisoners of war, which fought beside the Japanese at Imphal and Kohima in 1944.</blockquote><a href="/movement/indian-independence-movement">Indian independence movement · People</a></li><li><blockquote>There he took command of the <b>Indian National Army</b>, made up largely of Indian prisoners of war, and in October 1943 proclaimed the Provisional Government of Free India.</blockquote><a href="/state/azad-hind">Azad Hind</a></li><li><blockquote>Malays were courted while Indians were recruited into the <b>Indian National Army</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/japanese-occupation-of-malaya">Japanese occupation of Malaya</a></li></ul></section>
